_irNotes: {
	"decision-01": "01 — everything is tab, and that is the load-bearing decision rather than a shortcut. No Postgres, no Electric, no conduit, no bloblang, no migration, no policy, no publication, no pipeline. The reason is the grader, not the size: a raw pipeline has no browser-tier form at all, and the only check any app in this tree declares over a bloblang mapping is a lint. For an app whose oracle IS arithmetic, putting the arithmetic in the one seat no fixture harness reaches destroys the grader before it is written. Nothing is at device either: a device collection persists across program versions and nothing backfills a new column, so a returning reader would get binding {x} not in row on a schema that is certain to move"
	"decision-02": "02 — the aggregation is two reduces at the mutation seat. That seat is the one an off-disk mount executes, so every graded assertion runs at the test verb with no cluster up. Two rather than one because they have different worlds and different wake conditions: stitch depends on the log and the clock alone and is cursor-independent, rollup depends on the cursor and would otherwise re-derive the partition on every gesture. Splitting them is also what lets the visits be graded against DuckDB BEFORE any aggregate is, which is the one place a shared misunderstanding of the sessionization would show"
	"decision-03": "03 — a visit’s id is arithmetic, because a reduce has no post. A reduce may only write where it can name the key, so <visitor>#<ord> is not a convenience: it is the only way a derived row can exist at all. The same constraint gives a session-hour <session_id>@<hour> and a breakdown row <panel>|<value>. It costs the freedom to key on anything the log does not already determine, and it buys the property that a recomputation lands on the same rows rather than beside them"
	"decision-04": "04 — the clock is three integer rows and a civil-from-days algorithm. A reduce is denied Date, and the terminal’s only formatter is pinned to UTC, so a local day cannot be a rendered string. It is a subtraction of DATES over the Zone row covering the instant, and never k × 86400 seconds — across the clock change the two differ by an hour and that hour holds a hit. The independent witness is DuckDB’s ICU: the ground truth asks a real tz database the same question and the two must agree"
	"decision-05": "05 — every mutation is a form and every derivation is a fold; there is no data-on-click reduce anywhere. That removes the whole class of silently dead controls: a handler one level above its region still resolves, so a control wired one tag too high is inert under the finger with no error anywhere. A form either submits or it does not. The cost is that a gesture cannot compute — which is why a breakdown row carries the five set_* columns it would write (decision-13)"
	"decision-06": "06 — the five rules two implementations read differently are literals, not phrases. The boundary is the integer 1860 and appears as that integer in the fold, in the ground truth, in Metric.reads and in the test that walks it. Page views count only pageview; events count every hit except engagement. The entry page is the first page VIEWED. Duration runs to the last non-engagement hit. A bounce needs at most one page view AND no interactive non-pageview hit, read off the hit’s own column rather than off its name. “Thirty minutes” appears only in the published sentence, where it is a quotation and not a rule"
	"decision-07": "07 — the divisors are graded columns, not intermediates. sessions_n, bounce_denom, pv_sum and dur_sum sit beside the quotients they produce, and both are on the screen. Without them a wrong divisor hides behind a quotient that rounds the same, and the one case in the whole app where two metrics divide by DIFFERENT counts — bounce rate restricting its divisor to the visits that entered a matching page, while views per visit does not — would be invisible rather than demonstrated"
	"decision-08": "08 — the hourly visit counts do not sum to the day’s, and the screen says so rather than reconciling it. At hour granularity a visit is counted in every local hour it was open in, which is what SessionHour materialises, while visitors and page views are counted over hits. Two implementations can agree on every visit and still disagree here, so the asymmetry is stated in the ir, on the screen, and in an assertion that requires the two NOT to be equal. Presenting it as a sum would be the most plausible wrong app this brief admits"
	"decision-09": "09 — the wake graph closes on one edge, and the edge is a write order. A data-on-mutation seat wakes when its OWN region’s read changes and at no other time — data-reads fills the world and subscribes to nothing. So the second run of rollup, the one over a complete session set, exists only because stitch states corpus LAST and rollup keeps an ear on that row. Reordering those three writes silently gives rollup a partial world with no second wake to correct it, and every number would be zero with nothing red. Each mount settles at two runs of stitch and three of rollup; an assertion written to any other pair goes red on correct code"
	"decision-10": "10 — two cursor tables, and therefore no precedence branch. FilterSet is what a URL MEANS and is written by nothing; Filter is what the reader has done to it and is the only table a form writes. The reduce reads exactly one row of the second, through data-read-cursor=\"filter?id=eq.{param.preset}\", so there is no overlay to compose and no discriminator to go stale — a discriminator is a branch, and a stale one is a silently dead picker. This is also how a route parameter reaches arithmetic without a write, which no app in this tree does today. It costs the ability to name a cursor the eleven rows do not materialise, and that cost is what makes the answerable set finite"
	"decision-11": "11 — a panel’s SELECTION and ORDER are graded, not only its numbers. Ranked by page views instead of visitors, the page panel shows a different set of nine in a different order — and every number in it is a number this oracle answers, and answers correctly. So ord is a real column on the oracle’s side and in the coverage key on both sides. The tie leg decides membership rather than merely order: twenty-seven panels are tied on visitors across the ninth row, so a fixture that lost its ties would silently weaken this"
	"decision-12": "12 — the coherence guard lives in the reduce, because there is no store-side predicate to put it in. The platform has exactly three Jessie roles and none is a validator; #Entity declares no validations field; and a tab entity could not reach one anyway, since a refusal is an error off a mutation round trip a browser-tier collection never makes. So the writer guards its own writes: it fails at the same moment, in the seat where it happened, and costs a call. What is genuinely lost is a FORM’s write — the cursor is form-written and ungated, so a malformed cursor is caught by arithmetic rather than refused at the door. That is the honest cost, and it is what the platform is being asked for"
	"decision-13": "13 — a drill-down writes every slot, not one. upsertBy PATCHES an existing row with only the submitted fields, so a form carrying one f_* would leave the other four standing and a reader would reach a two-slot cursor no question grades — in one gesture, silently. Hence five set_* columns per breakdown and chip row, submitted together. They have to be real columns rather than bindings reached upward, because a nested item template binds against its own row and nothing else is in scope there"
	"decision-14": "14 — the chart is a leaf slot, and that is the design’s one real bet. A list region sweeps its markup on every refresh, so an authored element inside one cannot survive; a slot binds attributes and texts without sweeping, and across a root refresh the root’s item node is keyed by row id and survives, so the nested slot is re-stated rather than re-hydrated. <svg> in HTML content is the parser’s own breakout into foreign content, and d is neither a region attribute nor a URL attribute, so it takes the plain interpolate path. Nothing in this tree has a bound d yet, which is why the check that pins it is written before the chart is"
	"decision-15": "15 — every geometry number is a fold-computed column, never a string out of the log. The axis positions, the bar shares and the bounce arcs all ride custom properties, and nothing validates that channel: a value carrying its own ; is a second declaration nobody wrote. So each is an integer column with a stated range, which also puts the picture’s geometry on the oracle — the arc is graded like any other number"
	"decision-16": "16 — the citation and the claim are different columns. says is the published sentence and formula the published expression; reads is what this app actually computes. They sit in adjacent cells and are never merged, so rewording the app’s reading to match the specification would be a diff on a column that is supposed to be a quotation. Three of the seven formulas are the vendor’s own expressions transcribed character for character, and four separate facts — the zero-divisor answer, three roundings, and which divisor is conditional — are read off them rather than restated"
	"decision-17": "17 — which granularities a range allows is rows, not a guard. The seven legal pairs are Grain rows, and the granularity buttons come from a nested region filtered on the live range. An illegal pair is therefore not merely hidden: its button does not exist, so no transition names it and nothing has to refuse it. A rule inside a handler is not a coverage axis; rows are, and the coverage anti-join can ask whether every one of the seven was driven"
	"decision-18": "18 — no question row carries an expected value. A seeded expected value is a golden file, and a golden file grades the app against a number the same author wrote down while looking at the app. Every number comes from the ground truth at run time. The one class of number this author did not compute — the vendor’s own asserted values over transcribed cohorts — is not a question row either: those are assertions on the GROUND TRUTH, checked before the app is asked anything, because a ground truth that disagrees with the vendor is wrong regardless of what the app does"
	"decision-19": "19 — no auth, and the cost is stated. Nothing here is per-person: the log is a checked-in artifact rather than anybody’s traffic, and there is no second reader to keep apart from the first. Declining it costs the two things a real analytics product needs most — a site is somebody’s, and a dashboard is shared — and neither is approximable at this tier: they need the cluster, a passkey and row policies, which is a different app and not a bigger local store. Scoped out rather than faked"
	"decision-20": "20 — escape hatches: none, and one platform hole named instead. No container, no WASM unit, no vendored component, no external endpoint. The two pieces of app code are reduces in the terminal’s own compartment, with window, document, fetch, eval, Date and Math.random all denied. The hole worth naming is the other way round: a browser-tier route parameter is UNLINTABLE, because the visual battery resolves a parameter by fetching its planned table over PostgREST and an all-tab app has no such table. Both parametrized routes come back unresolved, each a major finding, and the route is dropped from the live set — so the battery stays green and photographs neither. Reading that green as coverage is the mistake; the two routes are opened by hand instead, once each, with the console open"
	"decision-21": "21 — the graded space is walked from BOTH sides, because one side is half an oracle. The reverse anti-join walks the numbers the app RENDERED, so it can only fire on something a screen showed: an app that shows nothing, or drops a whole panel, or cuts a panel to its top three rows, produces no finding anywhere in it. The forward anti-join does not close that gap either — it grades the question ROSTER and never sees a rendered or a graded position at all. So a third anti-join walks the oracle’s own answers and asks which of them no screen showed, and the gap it closes is measured rather than argued: before it existed, deleting an entire panel produced zero findings. There is no declined region on that side, because what the oracle chose to answer is exactly what some screen owes"
	"decision-22": "22 — there is no data-machine anywhere, and that is a finding rather than a preference. A chart narrows a transition as click@<dom-id> and the wiring lint resolves that id as a LITERAL, so no chart can answer a button generated from rows. “The legal pairs are rows” (decision-17) and “the transitions are narrowed to affordances” are therefore mutually exclusive at this tier, and only one of them can be had. The rows win: an illegal pair has no button to narrow to, and every gesture on the dashboard is a form like every other mutation in the app. What it costs is the platform’s own machines check, which walks every arrow of every emitted chart against XState — a whole check the app forfeits, stated here rather than left as an absence"
	"decision-23": "23 — the caret rides the chart’s singleton, not the point it is on. Two platform walls, one shape. data-rove would put the tabstop on the point row, and the terminal counts a row on its way out: the one gesture that shortens the axis past the caret leaves the departing row reading true beside the arriving one, and the refresh throws a set has one current member on correct app code. A region pinned on that point fails the other way — when the window stops holding it the terminal clears the region’s bindings, so the filter placeholder stands there unresolved while the forms write nothing, with no error anywhere. So the readout is the ONE tabstop, it hangs on Scale at id=eq.the whose read interpolates nothing, and the walk is caret_prev and caret_next off that row; the point rows carry on_caret as an ordinary bound attribute, where a stale true on a node about to vanish is a stale class and nothing more. aria-activedescendant is APG’s other focus model and the one this reaches. Both walls were found by performing them"
}

